Toxic & Iatrogenic Neuropathic Syndromes (CIPN Alcoholic Polyneuropathy Lead/Mercury/Arsenic Fluoroquinolones/Metronidazole)
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Symmetrical distal sensory loss, History of neurotoxic exposure, Symmetrical distal burning pain

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-050
Reason for Visit: Acute exacerbation of severe burning pain in the feet preventing sleep and causing frequent falls.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted severe, intractable burning pain in the lower extremities leading to total sleep deprivation and three unobserved falls today due to sensory ataxia. Urgent assessment is required to rule out acute spinal cord pathology, provide immediate potent neuropathic analgesia, and assess home safety before regular hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My feet are constantly on fire and completely numb at the same time. I keep falling over because I can't feel the floor."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a progressive onset of distal, strictly symmetrical stocking-and-glove sensory impairment. Over the last 48 hours, there has been a severe exacerbation of burning, tingling, and electric shock-like pain. The patient exhibits prominent sensory ataxia directly resulting in a severely unsteady gait, heavily exacerbated in the dark, leading to three falls today without head strike. The patient has a documented clinical history of recent exposure to a known chemotherapeutic agent (completed an Oxaliplatin-based regimen for colon cancer 4 weeks ago). The patient notes progressive, distal muscle weakness (mild foot drop). There are trophic skin changes including dry skin on the feet. The pain is severe enough to cause total insomnia.
Functional Status: Mobilizing with high risk of falls; using walls for support due to sensory ataxia.
Social/Safety Concerns: Living alone; high risk of a catastrophic fall and subsequent fracture; severe sleep deprivation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/85 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 36.7 C | RR: 16 bpm | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Fatigued, distressed, frequently shifting leg position due to severe discomfort.
Physical Exam: Neurological examination reveals the diagnostic tetrad: Symmetrical distal sensory loss (absent pinprick, light touch, and vibration sense up to the mid-calf), a clear history of neurotoxic exposure (Oxaliplatin), and symmetrical distal burning pain (allodynia on the dorsum of the feet). There are diminished or completely absent deep tendon reflexes initially at the ankles (absent Achilles reflexes bilaterally). Romberg test is strongly positive, confirming profound sensory ataxia. Motor exam shows mild weakness in ankle dorsiflexion (4/5 bilaterally). Peripheral pulses are bounding. No spinal tenderness.
Environmental Observations: Loose rugs on the floor; poor lighting in the hallway.
Medication Reconciliation: Paracetamol and Ibuprofen providing zero relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Chemotherapy-Induced Peripheral Neuropathy (CIPN) - Status: Acute Pain Exacerbation and Sensory Ataxia
Differentials:

Acute Lumbar Radiculopathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The symptoms are perfectly symmetrical and bilateral, strictly stocking-and-glove, rather than sharp, unilateral shooting pain down a single nerve root.

Diabetic Neuropathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The rapid, subacute onset immediately temporally linked to the administration of a known neurotoxic chemotherapeutic agent, though clinically the presentation is very similar.

Vitamin B12 Deficiency Neuropathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The clear recent chemotherapy exposure is the overwhelming primary driver, though B12 levels should be checked non-urgently to rule out a compounding factor.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient is suffering an acute, severe pain crisis and functional collapse secondary to chemotherapy-induced peripheral neuropathy. The profound sensory ataxia has caused multiple falls, rendering the patient unsafe to mobilize independently at home. Delaying assessment would result in unrelenting, agonizing pain and a near-certain catastrophic fall overnight. Urgent after-hours assessment definitively ruled out acute vascular or compressive spinal pathologies, allowing for the immediate initiation of targeted, membrane-stabilizing neuropathic agents and critical on-site environmental modifications to immediately secure the patient's physical safety, completely justifying an urgent intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed Pregabalin 75mg orally STAT, followed by 75mg at night. Prescribed a short course of Tapentadol 50mg PRN for severe breakthrough pain overnight.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Step 1: By actively leaving a bright light on in the hallway and bathroom all night, the patient entirely relies on their vision to balance, immediately compensating for the deadened nerves in the feet and preventing falls in the dark.
Step 2: By strictly wearing firm, rubber-soled shoes indoors instead of socks, the patient provides a massive increase in sensory feedback to the remaining functional nerves, instantly improving balance and preventing slipping.
Step 3: By removing all loose throw rugs completely from the walking paths, the patient physically eliminates the primary tripping hazards that catch the weakened, dropping feet.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Urgent referral to community physiotherapy for gait training and mobility aid assessment.
DME/Safety (Optional): Four-wheeled walker (rollator) recommended.
Follow-up: Routine GP and Oncology follow-up in 1 week to titrate neuropathic medications.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Proceed to the Emergency Department if you suffer a fall and hit your head, if you become entirely unable to bear weight on your legs, or if you develop new difficulty controlling your bowel or bladder.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: CIPN can be a chronic condition; complete pain resolution takes time and medication titration.
Clinical Course: The burning nerve pain is expected to lessen gradually over the next 1-2 weeks as the medication reaches therapeutic levels. Sleep should improve within 48 hours.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return for urgent review if the pain completely fails to respond, or if you notice any new, painless open sores on your feet.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The chemotherapy drugs that fought the cancer have unfortunately damaged the tiny sensory nerves in your feet. Because these nerves are damaged, they are sending false "burning" signals to the brain, and they are failing to tell your brain where your feet are, causing the falls. Standard painkillers do not work. We are starting a medication that calms these hyperactive nerves so you can sleep.
Actionable Steps: Take the Pregabalin 2 hours before bed. Leave a bright nightlight on. Remove all floor rugs immediately.

